content": "uphill"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "very difficult to be achieved"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Well, having pages designed with a close view of how they might be used to be an uphill struggle. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob05aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "look up to "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to admire"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Ever since this editor was developed, programmers looked up to it for the great job it does at optimizing the screen display for a particular type of output."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob05aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "aligned"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "organized having a straight line as a reference"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The layout mode, which shows something very similar to the final result with some few tools for the user to make sure everything is properly aligned and spaced."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob05aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "output"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "information generated by a computer"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Ever since this editor was developed, programmers looked up to it for the great job it does at optimizing the screen display for a particular type of output."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob05aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for examples of software using WYSIWYG editors. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write a text explaining the advantages and disadvantages of WYSIWYG editors in each software. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share with your partners. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I identify examples of WYSIWYG editors. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I explain the advantages and disadvantages of WYSIWYG editors in software. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share the information I've learned with others."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Causative With Get in Affirmative"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The <strong>causative</strong> is used to talk about something that someone did for us or for another person. <strong>Get</strong> is one of the most common verbs used with this kind of structure."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The <b>causative</b> is a passive form to mean that we (or someone else) paid, or ask someone to do something for us. We use the <b>causative</b> when we want to emphasize on the action done, rather than on the person who did the action.</br></br>  Example:</br><i>I <b>got</b> my web page designed with a WYSIWYG editor.</i></br></br>  We can use either <b>have</b> or <b>get</b> to from the <b>causative</b>, the difference is that <b>get</b> is less formal than <b>have</b>. </br></br>  The structure is: </br> <center>Subject (<b>the person who asked or paid for the action</b>) + get (<b>in the necessary tense</b>) + object (the person or thing that receives the action) + verb in past participle (<b>the action performed</b>) +complement.</center></br>  Examples: </br> <i>We are <b>getting</b> the program <b>fixed</b> by a WYSIWYG editor.</br> The company <b>got</b> the web page content <b>checked</b> last month.</i></br></br>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "WYSIWYG, which stands for \u201cWhat You See is What You Get\u201d, is a great tool to get codes translated into what final versions may look like.  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "The main advantage of WYSIWYG is that it saves time because the client can get changes made very easily."}, {"type": "example", "content": "But, its main feature is that designers can get results shown in three different modes. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Reported Speech with Ask"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The <strong>reported speech</strong> is used to say what another person said. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>We use <strong>reporting verbs</strong> to say what another person said.&nbsp;If we are reporting a question to another person, this stops being a question and becomes a statement. So, we need to change the grammar to an affirmative sentence.&nbsp;<br /> <br />In a <strong>question</strong>, we change the position of the subject and the verb.&nbsp;In a reported question, we change the order back to a positive question; therefore we no longer need a question mark. When the question begins with a&nbsp;<strong>Wh- word</strong>&nbsp;(what, where, which, who, how&hellip;), use that word as a connector:&nbsp;<br /> <br /> <strong><i>Direct speech:</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>what is WYSIWYG?<br /> <strong>Reported question:</strong>&nbsp;She asked me what WYSIWYG was.&nbsp;<br /> <br /> </i>However, if you have to report a yes/no question, instead of the Wh- word, you need to add&nbsp;<strong>if</strong>&nbsp;or&nbsp;<strong>whether:</strong>&nbsp;<br /> <br /> <strong><i>Direct Speech:</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>Is this WYSIWYG?<br /> <strong>Reported question:</strong>&nbsp;he asked me if this was WYSIWYG.&nbsp;<br /> <br /> </i>Don't forget to take into account the&nbsp;<strong>shift tense</strong>&nbsp;when reporting questions:</p>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":8,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\"><strong>DIRECT SPEECH</strong></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\"><strong>REPORTED SPEECH</strong></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Simple present</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Simple present (facts)</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Simple present</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Simple past</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Present continuous</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Past continuous</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Simple past</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Past Perfect</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Past continuous</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Past Perfect Continuous</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Present Perfect</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Past Perfect</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Past Perfect</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Past Perfect</div>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "He asked us what the WYSIWYG is.   "}, {"type": "example", "content": "@MelanieTech asked why WYSIWYG is such a towering figure in the world of computing.   "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Now, reader @TechnoGeek asked us what the main features of WYSIWYG editor system are. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The evolution of programming languages"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<strong>Programming languages</strong> are the basis of any software or application we use in our lives, let's learn a little about the history of programming languages."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Ada Lovelace is considered the first person to set the code for a machine to perform a particular work. Lovelace wrote and algorithm for Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine. Although this machine, and probably first computer, wasn't completed, it paved a path for programming languages. </br></br>  By 1970, the language known as Pascal was created by Niklaus Wirth. This language has been widely used for Windows Application Development and it is still used to make Skype desktop applications. In 1983, Bjarne Stroustrup developed the language C++; its object-oriented qualities have made it ideal for commercial application development, embedded software, server/client apps and video games. It currently powers Google's Chrome and some other similar web browsers.</br></br>  The 1990's witnessed an impressive boom of Language Programming development. Guido Van Rossum created the incredibly useful and powerful Python Language in 1991. Named after the British comedy group Monty Python, it is used for the development of web applications, software development, and information security, and it is currently used by Yahoo and Google. </br></br>  In 1993, Yukihiro Matsumoto released Ruby. Designed for productive and enjoyable programming, this language program is nowadays used in the development of applications like Twitter, Hulu and Groupon. James Gosling, from Sun Microsystems, created JAVA in 1995.  With its cross-platform functionality, this well-known programming language is primarily used for network programming, web application development, software development and graphical user interface development. It is most commonly used for the development of Android and OS apps. </br></br>  PHP, Hypertext Preprocessor, was also created in 1995 by Rasmus Lerdorf. Its main uses include building and maintaining dynamic web pages and server-side development. This language is mainly used by Facebook, Wikipedia, Digg, Wordpress, and Joomla. </br></br>  Another of the language programming breakthroughs of the 90's was the JAVASCRIPT. It was created to extend web page functionality and it is used by dynamic websites for form submission/validation, interactivity, user activity tracking, animations, among other functions. JAVASCRIPT is mostly used by Gmail, Adobe Photoshop and Mozilla Firefox.  "}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob05re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Interviewer:</strong> Hello! I'm Marie from the Techno Magazine. Today, we'll be tipping our hat to Anne with a Q&A session about WYSIWYG editing model with our twitter followers. Hello, Anne. Thank you so much for joining us today. </br>\n<strong>Anne:</strong> Hello! Thank YOU for the invitation.</br>\n<strong>Interviewer:</strong> Let's start with the first question from reader @WedDesign_Engineer. He asked us what the WYSIWYG is.  </br>\n<strong>Anne:</strong> As you know, codes are the foundation of computing. WYSIWYG, which stands for \u201cWhat You See is What You Get\u201d, is a great tool to get codes translated into what final versions may look like. Some examples are websites, printed documents, or slide presentations. The main advantage of WYSIWYG is that it saves time because the client can get changes made very easily.</br>"}, {"time": "64000", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob05re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Interviewer:</strong> Ok, let's move to our next question. @MelanieTech asked why WYSIWYG is such a towering figure in the world of computing. </br>\n<strong>Anne:</strong> I've been asked that a lot. Well, having pages designed with a close view of how they might be used to be an uphill struggle.  Ever since this editor was developed, programmers looked up to it for the great job it does at optimizing the screen display for a particular type of output. Even when Ada Lovelace wrote an algorithm for Charles Babbage, nothing had been as innovative as WYSIWYG. </br>"}, {"time": "105000", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob05re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Interviewer:</strong> That's quite interesting. Now, reader @TechnoGeek asked us what the main features of WYSIWYG editor system are.</br>\n<strong>Anne:</strong> Well, It's an editor that shows you in real time how the final version of your work will look like. But, its main feature is that designers can get results shown in three different modes: The composition mode, which shows a version very close to the final product with extra information and tools to modify it; it focuses more on composition than on layout. The layout mode, which shows something very similar to the final result with some few tools for the user to make sure everything is properly aligned and spaced. And thirdly, the preview mode, which presents the closest representation to the final result."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n8le01ob05re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n8le01ob05re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can explain what WYSIWYG is."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can give detailed information about WYSIWYG."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can identify key features relates to WYSIWYG."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "How can you see a preview of a webpage you are working on?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "How do you feel when you are updating a website with code and see that it is not working correctly?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "typ